<p>Most retail stores in Texas lease their space, so a storm or break-in usually creates two claims: the landlord's for the building and yours for everything inside it. The trouble starts when each side waits on the other. Your stock is ruined, the storefront is boarded, and the landlord's roofer hasn't been scheduled. We start with your lease, figure out what you're responsible for, and move your claim forward without waiting on the building claim.</p>

<h2>What your lease says about repairs</h2>
<p>Retail leases vary widely, and the lease controls who repairs and insures what. Look for:</p>
<ul class="checklist">
<li>The insurance clause: what the tenant must insure, and whether the landlord must be named</li>
<li>The repair and maintenance clause: storefront, glass, doors, HVAC, and roof responsibilities</li>
<li>The casualty clause: rent abatement, repair deadlines, and termination rights after damage</li>
<li>Who owns the improvements: whether your build-out becomes the landlord's property</li>
<li>Waiver of subrogation language, which affects whether insurers can pursue each other</li>
</ul>
<p>In many triple-net and shopping center leases, the tenant is responsible for the storefront glass, the interior, and its own HVAC unit, while the landlord insures the roof and structure. In others, the landlord handles more. Send us the lease with the policy and we will tell you which items belong in your claim.</p>

<h2>Storefront glass and doors</h2>
<p>Storefront systems are aluminum frames with large tempered or laminated panels, often with a glass door and closer. After hail, wind-borne debris, or a vehicle strike, the carrier may price only the broken pane. A complete storefront scope often includes:</p>
<ul>
<li>The glass, sized and specified correctly (tempered, laminated, or insulated)</li>
<li>Bent or damaged frame members and gaskets</li>
<li>Door hardware, closers, and locks</li>
<li>Emergency board-up and the return trip to remove it</li>
<li>Window graphics and vinyl lettering applied to the glass</li>
</ul>
<p>Some policies limit glass coverage or apply a separate deductible to it, so check the glass terms before assuming the full cost is covered.</p>

<h2>Signs</h2>
<p>Exterior signs take a lot of wind damage. Channel letters on the fascia, cabinet signs, and pylon or monument signs are expensive, and they're often covered differently from the building. Some commercial forms cap outdoor signs at a low amount unless they're scheduled, and a sign on a shared pylon may belong to the landlord. Get a written repair quote from a sign company, and note who owns each sign.</p>

[[cta]]

<h2>Stock and inventory</h2>
<p>Retail stock damaged by water, smoke, or a roof collapse is claimed as business personal property. A few points matter for retailers:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Valuation.</strong> Many policies value stock at cost, not retail price. Some include a selling price clause for sold but undelivered goods. Check which applies before you calculate the loss.</li>
<li><strong>Partial damage.</strong> Merchandise that is wet, smoky, or has damaged packaging may not be sellable at full price even if the product works. Document the condition and, where possible, get a salvage estimate.</li>
<li><strong>Records.</strong> Your point-of-sale system and purchase invoices are the strongest proof of what was on the shelves. Export the inventory report for the date of loss before counts change.</li>
<li><strong>Salvage.</strong> Don't throw anything out until it's been photographed and the insurer has had a chance to inspect or agree to disposal.</li>
</ul>
<p>Our <a href="/tools/contents-inventory-builder/">contents inventory builder</a> exports a CSV you can merge with your POS data.</p>

<h2>Short closure windows</h2>
<p>For most retailers, the business income claim is about days or weeks, not months, and every day closed shifts customers to competitors. That means the quickest path to reopening is usually also the best financial outcome. Things that help:</p>
<ol class="steps">
<li><strong>Mitigate the same day.</strong> Board up, stop water, and start drying. Reasonable emergency costs are usually part of the claim.</li>
<li><strong>Document before cleanup.</strong> Photos and video of every aisle and stockroom, with time stamps.</li>
<li><strong>Report to both insurers.</strong> Your carrier and, through the landlord, theirs.</li>
<li><strong>Track sales for the closure period.</strong> Daily sales from the same period last year help support the income claim, along with any extra costs to reopen, such as overtime or expedited restocking.</li>
</ol>
<p>Business income coverage, its waiting period, and any extra expense coverage all depend on your policy, so confirm what you have before making decisions based on it.</p>

<h2>How we work retail claims</h2>
<p>We inspect the space, write an Xactimate estimate for your improvements and storefront items, and build the stock and income claims from your records. When the landlord's roof claim affects your timeline, we stay in contact with the landlord's representatives so your repairs aren't held up longer than necessary. For water losses, see <a href="/services/water-damage-claims/">water damage claims</a>. How your tenant policy and the landlord's policy split a loss is covered in more general terms in our <a href="/commercial-claims/">commercial claims overview</a>.</p>
